    "answer": {
      "short": "The difference quotient factors into the difference quotient for $f$ at $g(x)$ and that for $g$ at $x$. Their limits give $y'=f'(g(x))g'(x)$.",
      "worked": "Put $u=g(x)$ and $\\Delta u=g(x+h)-g(x)$. As $h\\to0$, $\\Delta u\\to0$. For $\\Delta u\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(g(x+h))-f(g(x))}{h}\\\\y'&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(u+\\Delta u)-f(u)}{\\Delta u}\\frac{\\Delta u}{h}\\\\y'&=f'(u)g'(x)\\\\y'&=f'(g(x))g'(x).\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $\\Delta u=0$, the numerator is also zero; assigning the first quotient its limiting value $f'(u)$ gives the same result.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 62
  },
